FAST-LIO2 Rust core provenance

The algorithm sources in src/core/fast_lio were imported from the local
FAST-LIO-main snapshot of https://github.com/DoraCN/FAST-LIO (crate version
0.1.0). That snapshot has no Git metadata, so no upstream commit identifier
can be established from the supplied files. Hardware driver, map, navigation,
and application crates from that workspace are not included.

The Rust project attributes its port to FAST-LIO2, ikd-Tree and IKFoM/MTK:
https://github.com/hku-mars/FAST_LIO
https://github.com/hku-mars/ikd-Tree
https://github.com/hku-mars/IKFoM

The supplied Rust Cargo metadata and core README declare MIT OR Apache-2.0;
the supplied LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE are retained without alteration.
Its top-level README describes original ported-code notices as BSD-style,
whereas the official FAST_LIO repository has a GPL-2.0 LICENSE. These source
statements are inconsistent. This import does not resolve or supersede
upstream licensing: the redistribution authorization and required notices
must be clarified with the relevant authors before community publication.

Local changes for Dora integration are limited to standalone crate metadata,
fixed IMU/LiDAR time-offset handling, row-major rotation configuration, and
scan-end timestamps for result states, and read-only access to initialized-map
status, pending input counts, and registered/undistorted scans. Working scan
points are cleared before IMU processing so an empty IMU group cannot reuse
a prior scan. Per-line preprocessing buffers are resized to the configured
scan count at each message entry point instead of retaining the constructor
allocation for six scan lines. Direct Avia odometry mode no longer reserves
the entire frame in each unused scan-line buffer. After initialization, scans
with fewer than five effective scan-to-map correspondences do not enter the
local map; their result remains visible for the caller to reject/report.
No ROS runtime interfaces or hardware acquisition layer were imported.
